Falsifies `byte_sink_contract::verification::unpack_never_answers_more_than_a_message_holds`.

The defect: `unpack` lets a count one past the inline maximum through, so a message claiming
seventeen bytes decodes as seventeen.

Expected to fail: the harness's claim that `n <= INLINE_MAXIMUM`; Kani reports it failing at a
count of 17.

diff --git a/crates/byte_sink_contract/src/lib.rs b/crates/byte_sink_contract/src/lib.rs
index 7bf381b61..f111c5aa3 100644
--- a/crates/byte_sink_contract/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/byte_sink_contract/src/lib.rs
@@ -259,7 +259,7 @@ pub fn unpack(w0: u64, w1: u64, w2: u64, out: &mut [u8; INLINE_MAXIMUM]) -> Msg
     match operation(w0) {
         OPERATION_BYTES => {
             // Compared as `u64`, so a target whose `usize` is narrower cannot truncate the count.
-            if w0 & 0xffff_ffff > INLINE_MAXIMUM as u64 || kind(w0) != KIND_TEXT {
+            if w0 & 0xffff_ffff > INLINE_MAXIMUM as u64 + 1 || kind(w0) != KIND_TEXT {
                 return Msg::Malformed;
             }
             let n = len(w0);
